VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Spouse hours aggregation for material participation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Carlton and Diane file a joint return for 2026. Carlton participates 300 hours and Diane participates 250 hours in the same business activity during the year. No employee participates more hours than either spouse. Under the Section 469 material participation rules, how are their participation hours treated?', '["Only the higher-participating spouse''s 300 hours are counted toward the test", "Each spouse must independently satisfy a material participation test to avoid passive treatment", "Their 550 combined hours satisfy the 500-hour material participation test on the joint return", "Combined hours only count if both spouses each participate at least 100 hours"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-5T(f)(3), a spouse''s participation is attributed to the other spouse for purposes of the material participation tests on a joint return. Carlton''s 300 hours plus Diane''s 250 hours yield 550 combined hours, exceeding the 500-hour threshold of Test 1. Choice B is incorrect because the regulations aggregate spousal hours rather than testing each spouse independently.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Self-rental recharacterization rule
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Brennan owns a commercial building that he rents to his S corporation, which operates a consulting firm in which Brennan materially participates. The rental generates $60,000 of income during 2026. Under the Section 469 self-rental rules, how is the $60,000 rental income classified?', '["Passive income available to offset Brennan''s other passive losses", "Nonpassive income recharacterized under the self-rental rule", "Portfolio income excluded from the passive activity computation", "Active business income reported on the S corporation''s return"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-2(f)(6), rental income from property rented to a trade or business in which the taxpayer materially participates is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This prevents taxpayers from generating artificial passive income to absorb passive losses. Choice A is incorrect because the self-rental rule specifically overrides the default passive classification when the taxpayer materially participates in the lessee activity.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Definition of rental activity under Section 469
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What is the general definition of a rental activity under Section 469?', '["Only real property leased for 12 months or more qualifies as a rental activity", "Any activity in which the taxpayer charges rent to customers is automatically classified as rental", "An activity is rental only if customers use the tangible property for more than 30 days on average", "An activity where payments are principally for the use of tangible property is generally a rental activity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3), a rental activity is one where payments are principally for the customer''s use of tangible property, whether real or personal. The definition is broad and not limited to real property or a specific lease duration. Choice A is incorrect because the definition encompasses both real and personal property with no minimum lease term requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Recharacterization of significant participation activity income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Novak participates 180 hours each in three separate business activities during 2026, none of which he materially participates in individually. Activity A generates $50,000 of income, Activity B produces a $30,000 loss, and Activity C generates $10,000 of income. Under the significant participation activity recharacterization rules, how is the net $30,000 of income treated?', '["The net $30,000 is recharacterized as nonpassive income under the significant participation rules", "The net $30,000 is passive income available to offset passive losses from other activities", "Each activity must be treated separately so only Activity B''s $30,000 loss is suspended", "All income is treated as active business income because participation exceeds 100 hours per activity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-2T(f)(2), net income from significant participation passive activities (100+ hours but not material participation) is recharacterized as nonpassive income. Novak''s three activities net to $30,000 of income ($50K + $10K − $30K), which is recharacterized. Choice B is incorrect because the recharacterization rule specifically prevents this income from sheltering other passive losses.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Equipment rental classified as passive, no $25K allowance
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Whitfield owns heavy construction equipment that she leases to contractors under long-term leases. She does not materially participate in managing the rental business but actively participates in management decisions. The activity generates $35,000 of losses. Whitfield has AGI of $95,000 and no passive income. How much of the rental loss can Whitfield deduct?', '["$35,000, because she actively participates in the rental activity", "$25,000, under the special rental loss allowance for active participants", "$0, because the Section 469(i) special allowance applies only to rental real estate activities", "$10,000, after applying the AGI phase-out to the $25,000 allowance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(i), the $25,000 special allowance for active participants applies only to rental real estate activities, not to rentals of personal property such as equipment. Because Whitfield''s equipment rental is not real estate, the entire $35,000 loss is suspended as a passive loss. Choice B is incorrect because the special allowance is limited to rental real estate, not all rental activities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Former passive activity: suspended loss treatment
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Prescott had $50,000 of suspended passive losses from a dry-cleaning business in which he did not materially participate from 2023 through 2025. In 2026, Prescott begins materially participating and the business generates $30,000 of income. Under Section 469, how are the prior suspended losses treated?', '["The full $50,000 of prior suspended losses can offset any income, including wages and portfolio income", "Up to $30,000 of suspended losses can offset income from the formerly passive activity only", "The suspended losses remain frozen and can only offset income from other passive activities", "The suspended losses are permanently disallowed once the activity becomes nonpassive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-1(f)(4), when a formerly passive activity becomes nonpassive, suspended losses may offset income from that specific activity but not other nonpassive income. Prescott can use $30,000 of the $50,000 suspended losses against the activity''s income, with $20,000 remaining suspended. Choice A is incorrect because formerly passive losses do not become fully deductible against all income sources.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Publicly traded partnership loss isolation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Thornton invests in a publicly traded partnership (PTP) that generates a $25,000 passive loss during 2026. He also earns $40,000 of passive income from a private real estate limited partnership. Under the Section 469 PTP rules, how is the $25,000 PTP loss treated?', '["The $25,000 PTP loss offsets $25,000 of the private partnership''s passive income", "The $25,000 PTP loss can offset any passive income regardless of source", "The $25,000 PTP loss is permanently disallowed because PTPs cannot generate deductible losses", "The $25,000 PTP loss is suspended and can only offset future income from the same PTP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(k), passive losses from a publicly traded partnership can only offset income from that same PTP. They cannot be netted against passive income from other activities. The $25,000 loss is suspended and carried forward until the PTP generates income or Thornton fully disposes of the interest. Choice A is incorrect because PTP losses are ring-fenced from all other passive income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — At-risk recapture when amount goes negative
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hargrove invested $150,000 cash in a business activity and claimed $120,000 of losses over three years, reducing her at-risk amount to $30,000. At the start of 2026, she receives a $50,000 distribution from the activity, reducing her at-risk amount to negative $20,000. Under Section 465(e), what is the tax consequence?', '["Hargrove must include $20,000 of income, recapturing prior losses to restore her at-risk amount to zero", "No income is recognized because at-risk recapture applies only upon disposition of the activity", "The entire $50,000 distribution is treated as capital gain under Section 731", "Hargrove must amend prior returns to reduce previously claimed losses by $20,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 465(e), if a taxpayer''s at-risk amount is reduced below zero (here by the distribution exceeding her remaining at-risk amount), the taxpayer must recapture previously deducted losses as income to the extent the amount goes negative. Hargrove includes $20,000 of ordinary income, resetting her at-risk amount to zero. Choice B is incorrect because recapture is triggered whenever the at-risk amount drops below zero, not only upon disposition.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Seven-day average rental period exclusion
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kessler owns a vacation cabin near a ski resort that she rents to guests for an average of 5 days per rental period during 2026. She provides daily maid service and fresh linens. Kessler does not materially participate in the activity. Under the Section 469 regulations, how is this activity classified?', '["As a passive rental activity subject to the $25,000 active participation allowance", "As a per se passive activity because it involves rental of real property", "As an activity excluded from the rental activity definition because the average rental period is 7 days or less", "As portfolio income because short-term rentals are not trade or business activities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(A), an activity is not treated as a rental activity if the average period of customer use is 7 days or less. Because Kessler''s cabin averages 5 days per rental, it falls outside the rental activity definition and is instead classified as a trade or business. Choice A is incorrect because the 7-day exception removes the activity from rental treatment entirely.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Grouping consistency requirement
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Morrison grouped three retail store activities together as a single activity in 2024 for passive activity purposes. In 2026, she wants to separate them into individual activities because one store is profitable while the other two generate losses. No material change in facts and circumstances has occurred. Under the Section 469 grouping regulations, can Morrison regroup?', '["Yes, taxpayers may freely regroup passive activities in any tax year", "No, once activities are grouped the grouping cannot be changed absent a material change in facts and circumstances", "Yes, but only if the taxpayer obtains written IRS consent before the filing deadline", "Yes, regrouping is automatically permitted every three tax years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-4(e), once a taxpayer groups activities, the grouping is generally binding for subsequent years unless a material change in facts and circumstances makes the grouping clearly inappropriate. Morrison cannot regroup solely for tax benefit without such a change. Choice A is incorrect because the consistency requirement prevents taxpayers from regrouping at will to manipulate passive income and loss netting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Active participation vs. material participation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What is the key difference between active participation and material participation under Section 469?', '["Active participation is a lower standard requiring bona fide involvement in management decisions", "Active participation requires 500 or more hours of annual participation in the activity", "Material participation is a lower standard that only rental property owners must satisfy", "There is no practical difference between the two standards in the regulations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(i), active participation is a lower threshold than material participation, requiring only bona fide involvement in management decisions such as approving tenants, setting rental terms, or authorizing repairs. Material participation under Reg. 1.469-5T requires meeting one of seven specific tests, including the 500-hour test. Choice B is incorrect because the 500-hour threshold applies to material participation, not active participation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Passive activity credits limited to tax on passive income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Dawson has $15,000 of passive activity tax credits from a business equipment leasing partnership and $5,000 of regular tax liability attributable to his passive activity income from other sources. He has no rental real estate activities. Under Section 469(d)(2), how much of the credits can Dawson use in 2026?', '["$15,000, because passive credits may offset any regular tax liability", "$0, because credits from equipment leasing partnerships are permanently disallowed", "$15,000, because the Section 469(i) special allowance covers the excess credits", "$5,000, limited to the tax attributable to passive activity income, with the $10,000 excess suspended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(d)(2), passive activity credits can only offset the tax allocable to passive activity income. Dawson''s $5,000 of tax on passive income caps his current credit usage; the remaining $10,000 is suspended and carried forward. Choice A is incorrect because passive credits, unlike general business credits, are restricted to the tax generated by passive income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Related party sale does not release suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Fielding sells her entire interest in a passive activity to her brother for $120,000 (FMV) in a fully taxable transaction. She has a $90,000 basis and $45,000 of suspended passive losses. Under Section 469(g), what happens to the suspended losses?', '["The $45,000 is fully deductible because Fielding disposed of her entire interest", "The suspended losses transfer to her brother along with the carryover basis", "The suspended losses remain suspended because the sale is to a related party under Section 267", "Only the $30,000 gain can be offset by suspended losses, with the remaining $15,000 permanently disallowed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(g)(1)(B), the rule allowing full deduction of suspended losses upon a complete disposition does not apply when the transferee is a related party under Section 267(b). Siblings are related parties under Section 267(b)(1), so the $45,000 remains suspended. Choice A is incorrect because the related-party exception overrides the general complete-disposition release rule.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Self-charged interest recharacterization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Garrison owns 100% of an S corporation that is a passive activity generating $80,000 of income. Garrison loans $200,000 to the S corporation at 6% interest, receiving $12,000 of interest income in 2026. Under Reg. 1.469-7, how is the $12,000 interest income classified?', '["As portfolio income excluded from the passive activity computation", "As passive income to the extent of the S corporation''s passive activity deductions for the interest", "As active business income because the taxpayer owns 100% of the entity", "As self-employment income subject to FICA taxes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-7, self-charged interest income from a pass-through entity is recharacterized as passive income to the extent the entity has passive deductions attributable to the interest expense. This prevents conversion of passive income into portfolio income through related-party lending. Choice A is incorrect because the self-charged interest rule overrides the default portfolio income classification for this lending arrangement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Phase-out computation for $25K allowance
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Patterson has AGI of $120,000 and a $25,000 rental real estate loss from a property in which she actively participates during 2026. Under the Section 469(i) phase-out rules, how much of the rental loss can Patterson deduct?', '["$15,000", "$20,000", "$25,000", "$10,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(i)(3), the $25,000 special allowance is reduced by 50% of AGI exceeding $100,000. Patterson''s phase-out is ($120,000 − $100,000) × 50% = $10,000, reducing the allowance from $25,000 to $15,000. Choice D ($10,000) is incorrect because it represents the phase-out reduction amount, not the remaining deductible allowance.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Spouse participation on joint return scenario
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Fleming works full-time as an engineer with 2,000 hours and participates 200 hours in a restaurant activity during 2026. His wife Gloria participates 350 hours in the same restaurant. They file jointly and own the restaurant equally. Under Reg. 1.469-5T(f)(3), what is the activity''s participation status?', '["The activity is passive because neither spouse independently meets the 500-hour test", "Gloria''s participation qualifies her individually but Fleming is still treated as passive", "Only Fleming''s hours count because he is the partner of record on the ownership documents", "Their 550 combined hours satisfy the 500-hour material participation test on the joint return"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-5T(f)(3), participation by a taxpayer''s spouse is treated as participation by the taxpayer for material participation purposes on a joint return. Fleming''s 200 hours plus Gloria''s 350 hours total 550 hours, exceeding the 500-hour threshold. Choice A is incorrect because the regulations require combining spousal hours rather than testing each spouse separately.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Installment sale and proportional suspended loss release
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Everett sells his entire interest in a passive activity on January 1, 2026, for $300,000, receiving $100,000 down and a $200,000 note payable in two equal annual installments. His basis is $180,000 and he has $60,000 of suspended passive losses. The gross profit ratio is 40%. Under Section 469(g)(3), how are the suspended losses treated in 2026?', '["The full $60,000 is deductible in 2026 because the entire interest was sold", "No suspended losses are allowed until the final installment payment is received", "$20,000 of suspended losses is allowed in 2026, proportional to the installment gain recognized", "$60,000 offsets the $40,000 gain with $20,000 deductible against other income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469(g)(3), when a passive activity is disposed of via installment sale, suspended losses are released proportionally as gain is recognized. Year-one gain: $100,000 × 40% = $40,000, which is one-third of total $120,000 gain. Suspended losses allowed: $60,000 × ($40,000 ÷ $120,000) = $20,000. Choice A is incorrect because the installment method defers both gain recognition and the proportional release of suspended losses.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — At-risk amount components
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Warren contributes $75,000 cash to a business activity. She also borrows $40,000 from a bank on a full-recourse personal loan and contributes those proceeds to the activity. Under Section 465, what is Warren''s at-risk amount?', '["$40,000", "$115,000", "$75,000", "$150,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 465(b), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount includes cash contributions and amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer has personal liability (recourse debt). Warren''s $75,000 cash plus $40,000 recourse loan totals $115,000 at risk. Choice C ($75,000) is incorrect because it omits the recourse borrowing, which is included in the at-risk computation when the taxpayer bears personal liability.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Working interest in oil and gas exception
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What type of interest in oil and gas property is exempt from passive activity loss limitations under Section 469(c)(3)?', '["A working interest not held through a limited partnership or other entity limiting the holder''s liability", "A royalty interest in any producing oil and gas well", "An overriding royalty interest in oil and gas production", "Any interest in an oil and gas activity regardless of entity structure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(c)(3), a working interest in an oil and gas property is excluded from passive activity treatment provided the taxpayer''s liability is not limited by the entity form. This exception applies regardless of material participation. Choice B is incorrect because royalty interests generate portfolio income and are not covered by the working interest exception.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: hard / L3 — Net investment income tax on passive income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Dr. Nakamura, a single filer, has 2026 MAGI of $350,000, comprising $250,000 of W-2 income, $60,000 of net passive rental income, and $40,000 of portfolio income. Under Section 1411, what amount of her income is subject to the 3.8% net investment income tax?', '["$60,000, because only passive rental income triggers the NIIT", "$150,000, the full amount of MAGI exceeding the $200,000 single-filer threshold", "$40,000, because only portfolio income is classified as net investment income", "$100,000, the lesser of net investment income and the MAGI excess over the $200,000 threshold"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 1411, the 3.8% NIIT applies to the lesser of net investment income or MAGI exceeding $200,000 (single). Net investment income includes passive income ($60,000) and portfolio income ($40,000), totaling $100,000. The MAGI excess is $150,000. The lesser amount is $100,000. Choice B is incorrect because the NIIT applies to the lesser of the two amounts, not the full MAGI excess.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Personal service corporation passive loss treatment
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Apex Medical Consulting, P.C., a personal service corporation, has $200,000 of active consulting income and $80,000 of passive losses from a rental property investment during 2026. Under Section 469(a)(2), how are the passive losses treated?', '["The passive losses are suspended and can only offset the corporation''s future passive income", "The $80,000 passive loss offsets $80,000 of consulting income, reducing taxable income to $120,000", "The passive losses are permanently disallowed for personal service corporations", "The $80,000 offsets consulting income because all C corporations are exempt from Section 469"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(a)(2), personal service corporations are subject to the passive activity rules and cannot offset active service income with passive losses. The $80,000 is suspended until the corporation earns passive income or disposes of the activity. Choice B is incorrect because, unlike closely held C corporations under Section 469(e)(2), personal service corporations cannot use passive losses against active business income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Rental activity exception: extraordinary personal services
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Which of the following situations is excluded from the rental activity definition under the Section 469 regulations?', '["A taxpayer renting a commercial office building under a five-year lease", "A taxpayer renting farmland to a neighboring farmer under a two-year lease", "A taxpayer providing hospital rooms where extraordinary personal services are rendered to patients", "A taxpayer renting a warehouse to a single tenant on a month-to-month basis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(B), an activity is not treated as a rental activity if extraordinary personal services are provided in making the property available for use. Hospital rooms are a classic example because the value lies in the medical services, not the room itself. Choice A is incorrect because a standard commercial lease involves no extraordinary personal services and remains a rental activity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Short-term boat rental reclassified as trade or business
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kemp operates a boat rental business at a lake resort, renting fishing boats to customers for an average period of 3 days per rental. Kemp participates 800 hours per year in the activity. Under the Section 469 rental activity exceptions, how is this activity classified?', '["As a rental activity because boats are tangible property provided for customer use", "As a nonpassive trade or business because the average rental period is 7 days or less and Kemp materially participates", "As a passive activity regardless of rental period because it involves personal property rentals", "As a rental activity eligible for the $25,000 active participation allowance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(A), an activity is not a rental activity if the average customer use period is 7 days or less. Kemp''s 3-day average removes the activity from rental classification, making it a trade or business. Because Kemp participates 800 hours (exceeding 500), the activity is nonpassive. Choice A is incorrect because the 7-day rule overrides the general rental classification.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Multiple limitation layers on limited partner losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Ingram invests $100,000 cash in a limited partnership that acquires a building with $600,000 of qualified nonrecourse bank financing under Section 465(b)(6). Ingram''s 25% share gives her a $250,000 debt allocation. In year one, the partnership allocates $200,000 of losses to Ingram. She has no passive income and AGI of $200,000. As a limited partner she does not actively participate. What is Ingram''s maximum deductible loss?', '["$200,000, because qualified nonrecourse financing provides sufficient at-risk and basis amounts", "$25,000, under the active participation rental real estate allowance", "$150,000, limited to the qualified nonrecourse financing amount", "$0, because the passive activity rules suspend the entire loss with no passive income and no applicable exception"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469 and Section 465(b)(6), Ingram''s basis ($350,000) and at-risk amount ($350,000 with qualified nonrecourse financing) both exceed the $200,000 loss, but the passive activity rules are the binding constraint. As a limited partner without active participation and AGI above $150,000, the $25,000 allowance is unavailable and fully phased out. With no passive income, all $200,000 is suspended. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the passive activity limitation layer.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Limited partner material participation tests
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under the Section 469 temporary regulations, which material participation test is available to a limited partner?', '["The 100-hour significant participation test combined with other activities", "The 500-hour participation test, one of three tests available to limited partners", "The facts and circumstances test based on regular and continuous involvement", "The test requiring participation for substantially all hours in the activity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-5T(e)(2), limited partners may establish material participation only through three of the seven tests: (1) 500+ hours, (5) material participation in 5 of 10 preceding years, or (6) personal service activity with participation in 3 preceding years. Choice C is incorrect because the facts-and-circumstances test is not available to limited partners under the temporary regulations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Grouping election strategy for RE professional
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Real estate professional Dominique owns four rental properties. She materially participates 200 hours each in Properties 1 and 2 (combined $50,000 income) but only 80 hours each in Properties 3 and 4 (combined $90,000 losses). Without a grouping election, Properties 3 and 4 are passive. If Dominique makes a Section 469(c)(7)(A) election to treat all four as a single activity, what is the result?', '["Grouping combines all hours to 560, satisfying the 500-hour test, so the net $40,000 loss is fully deductible as nonpassive", "Grouping changes nothing because each property must be separately tested for material participation", "Grouping allows netting but the loss remains passive because grouped rentals need 750 hours", "The election is unavailable because Dominique does not materially participate in every individual property"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469(c)(7)(A), a qualifying real estate professional may elect to treat all rental real estate interests as a single activity. Dominique''s combined 560 hours exceeds the 500-hour material participation threshold for the grouped activity, making the net $40,000 loss ($50K income − $90K losses) nonpassive and fully deductible. Choice B is incorrect because the grouping election specifically allows aggregate testing rather than property-by-property evaluation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Three-layer limitation stacking: basis, at-risk, passive
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Rivera is a 30% limited partner with an outside basis of $80,000, at-risk amount of $60,000, and $15,000 of suspended passive losses from prior years. The partnership allocates a $100,000 loss to Rivera in 2026. She has $20,000 of passive income from another activity. Determine the total loss Rivera can deduct after applying all three limitation layers.', '["$60,000, because the at-risk limitation is the binding constraint", "$80,000, because only the basis limitation applies to limited partners", "$20,000, because after basis limits the loss to $80,000 and at-risk further limits it to $60,000, only $20,000 of passive income is available to absorb losses", "$75,000, combining the current allowed loss with prior suspended losses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 469 and Section 465, the three limitations apply sequentially. Basis: $100,000 loss capped at $80,000. At-risk: $80,000 capped at $60,000. Passive: $60,000 current plus $15,000 prior suspended totals $75,000 of passive losses against only $20,000 of passive income, so $20,000 is deductible. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the passive activity limitation that further restricts the at-risk-allowed amount.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Passive loss carryforward rules
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What happens to disallowed passive activity losses under Section 469?', '["They are permanently lost and cannot be deducted in any future year", "They may be carried back two years to offset prior passive income", "They expire after five years if not used against passive income", "They are suspended and carried forward indefinitely until offset by passive income or released upon a qualifying disposition"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 469(b), disallowed passive activity losses are suspended and carried forward indefinitely. They may be used in future years against passive income from any activity or fully released upon a complete taxable disposition to an unrelated party under Section 469(g). Choice C is incorrect because there is no expiration period for suspended passive losses.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Bed-and-breakfast with significant services: non-rental
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Ortega operates a bed-and-breakfast where guests stay an average of 4 days per visit. She provides daily breakfast, housekeeping, and concierge services, and participates 800 hours annually. Under the Section 469 rental activity exception regulations, how is this activity classified?', '["As a nonpassive trade or business because the average rental period is 7 days or less with significant services and Ortega materially participates", "As a passive rental activity because guests pay for the use of lodging", "As a passive activity because all lodging operations are classified as per se passive", "As a rental activity eligible for the $25,000 active participation allowance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(A), activities with an average customer use period of 7 days or less are excluded from the rental activity definition. Additionally, under subparagraph (B), significant personal services further support non-rental treatment. Since Ortega materially participates (800 hours), the activity is nonpassive. Choice B is incorrect because the 7-day and significant-services exceptions remove the activity from rental classification.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L2 — Grouping as an appropriate economic unit
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Bishop owns a retail clothing store and a dry-cleaning business that share the same building, the same employees, and many of the same customers. Under the Section 469 grouping regulations, what is the most appropriate treatment?', '["The two businesses must always be treated as separate activities because they are different industries", "Bishop may group the two businesses as a single activity if they form an appropriate economic unit based on common ownership, location, and customers", "The businesses are automatically grouped because they share employees", "Grouping is only permitted for rental activities, not for trade or business activities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 469 and Reg. 1.469-4(c), a taxpayer may group activities into a single activity if they constitute an appropriate economic unit, considering factors such as common control, common ownership, geographic location, and business interdependencies. Bishop''s shared building, employees, and customers strongly support grouping. Choice A is incorrect because different industry types do not preclude grouping when economic integration factors are present.', 'easy', 2)
;

COMMIT;
